course: dictionary
lesson: 14628
title: A word from old books
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Old books sometimes use words that nobody says today. «The travellers settled by the fire for a long evening of potations.» Look at the clues: they settled by the fire, it was a long evening, and they were at an inn. Potations must be things people drink there, so it means drinks.
explain: Potation is an old, formal word. It can mean a drink, especially an alcoholic one, or the act of drinking. Today we just say «drink». Ask: what word would fit here? Then swap in drink and check that the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Writers in older books sometimes used potation for a bit of comedy, because it sounds grander than a simple drink. When you meet a word like this, use the clues around it first, and check a dictionary if you are still not sure.
fact: Potation means a drink, especially an alcoholic drink.
fact: Potation can also mean the act of drinking.
fact: Potation is an old, formal word, so you will mostly find it in older books.
fact: Today we say «drink» instead of potation.
exercise: «The travellers sat by the fire, enjoying their potations.» What does potations mean here? | Blankets | *Drinks | Horses | Songs
exercise: Find and copy the word from this sentence that means a drink: «The old inn served a fine potation to every guest.» | *potation | guest | cups | served
exercise: Mum says potation is old-fashioned. What word would you use today? | Kitchen | *Drink | Chair | Sandwich
test: «After many long potations, the old innkeeper fell asleep behind the bar.» What does potations mean in this sentence? | Sleep | *Drinks | Windows | Bar counter
test: Ella finds the word potation in a book from 1850. What is the best way to understand it? | Guess it is a kind of dog | *Use the clues around it, then check a dictionary | Put the book away for good | Skip it, it is not important
exam: Which word would a modern writer use instead of potation? | Feast | *Drink | Walk | Blanket
